WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.260
The ending of "The Last of Us" season&nbsp;
one came as a shock to the uninitiated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.900
but those in-the-know were&nbsp;
bracing for it all along,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:10.800
when they weren't too busy keeping their&nbsp;
eyes peeled for Easter eggs. And there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.972
were plenty for fans to find. Spoilers for&nbsp;
both "The Last of Us" games and show ahead!

00:00:15.972 --> 00:00:21.060
The finale of Season 1 opens with Ashley Johnson,&nbsp;
the voice of Ellie in the games, running into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:25.800
an abandoned farmhouse to get away from an&nbsp;
infected runner. In both games, Johnson brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:30.480
the character of Ellie to life. In the show, she&nbsp;
plays Anna, who is revealed to be Ellie's mother.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:35.400
Throughout the cold open, in which Anna fights off&nbsp;
an infected mere moments after her water breaks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.600
hearing her talk and grunt is surreal, as&nbsp;
it's impossible for fans of the games not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.720
to picture the digital version&nbsp;
of Ellie. Anna is bitten by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:46.440
runner who follows her into the house,&nbsp;
and Ellie is born during the struggle.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:50.940
The source of Ellie's immunity is never revealed&nbsp;
in the game, though it's implied that there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:55.800
something irregular about how cordyceps mutates in&nbsp;
her brain, which is why the Fireflies must perform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:00.900
a fatal surgery on her to develop a cure. In the&nbsp;
finale, we learn that Ellie's immunity likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:05.160
stems from the fact that she was still attached&nbsp;
to Anna's umbilical cord when Anna was bitten.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:09.120
Ashley Johnson playing Ellie's mother is&nbsp;
pretty perfect, but if that wasn't enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:13.560
Laura Bailey, who plays Abby in "The Last of&nbsp;
Us Part II," plays one of the three masked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:18.780
nurses in the operating room. No spoilers, but&nbsp;
fans of "Part II" know Abby's connection to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:24.276
that operating room and what goes down in it,&nbsp;
making Bailey's cameo a heartbreaking touch.

00:01:24.276 --> 00:01:29.700
There are several recognizable motifs, themes,&nbsp;
and objects in "The Last of Us," and many of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:34.080
are shown in the finale. We finally get to see&nbsp;
the pivotal giraffe scene that has been teased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:39.180
all season long, and there is the same harrowing&nbsp;
final story beat when Ellie says "Okay" to Joel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:44.040
near Jackson. One iconic weapon, however, finally&nbsp;
gets a well-earned backstory early in the episode.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:48.600
At the beginning of the episode, Ellie's&nbsp;
pregnant mother Anna fights off an infected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.140
runner with a knife. The knife is actually&nbsp;
the switchblade that Ellie has been carrying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:55.620
for years — a piece of information&nbsp;
we never learn in the games. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:58.800
Anna only appears in the game&nbsp;
via a letter she left for Ellie.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:03.900
Over the course of the two games, the one constant&nbsp;
in Ellie's life is her switchblade. Now that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:08.940
know it originally belonged to her mother, it&nbsp;
carries even more weight in the franchise's lore.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:13.860
At the heart of "The Last of Us Part II" is&nbsp;
Ellie's relationship with Dina, a girl her age who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:18.540
came to Jackson before her and Joel. In the first&nbsp;
game, Dina doesn't make any appearances and is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:23.220
never mentioned, but it's quite possible that we&nbsp;
got to see her briefly in Episode 6 of the show.

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:27.420
After arriving in Jackson, Ellie and Joel&nbsp;
are given a proper meal from Tommy and Maria.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:32.160
While she's chowing down, Ellie yells at a girl&nbsp;
lurking behind a wooden post in the dining hall.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:35.880
"What!?"
"What's wrong with you?"&nbsp;

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:38.660
"What about her manners?"
"She was just curious."

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:43.080
The girl looks like Dina, and the interaction&nbsp;
parallels a conversation she has with Ellie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.556
in the second game, when they reminisce&nbsp;
about Ellie first getting to Jackson:

00:02:46.675 --> 00:02:50.215
"Stuffing food in all my pockets for&nbsp;
later like it was gonna run out."&nbsp;

00:02:50.367 --> 00:02:51.687
"I saw that!"

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:56.640
It hasn't been confirmed yet whether or not&nbsp;
the girl was Dina, but showrunner Craig Mazin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:00.428
all-but-confirmed it on an episode of&nbsp;
the official "The Last of Us" podcast.

00:03:00.428 --> 00:03:03.780
"I wonder who that could be,&nbsp;
theoretically, or not. We'll find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:04.985
out maybe one day."
"Oh!"

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:09.660
While touring Jackson in Episode 6, Maria&nbsp;
takes Ellie and Joel to the stables,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:12.346
where they get to meet a foal&nbsp;
that goes by a familiar name.

00:03:12.346 --> 00:03:14.160
"You want to pet her?"
"Yeah! What's her name?"&nbsp;

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:15.780
"Shimmer."
"Shimmer!"

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:20.820
Fans of the games surely perked their heads up at&nbsp;
that detail, as Shimmer is the horse that Dina and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:25.440
Ellie take when they go to Seattle in "The Last&nbsp;
of Us Part II." When we meet Shimmer in the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:30.000
she's a fully grown horse, so getting to&nbsp;
see her as a youngling is a sweet touch.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:34.980
Early on in Episode 7, as Ellie is about to&nbsp;
go to bed during the FEDRA school flashback,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:38.760
there is a quick shot of a stack of&nbsp;
cassette tapes on her desk. On top,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:43.020
we see A-ha's greatest hits compilation.&nbsp;
Beneath it, there's an Etta James album.

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:46.860
Both are Easter eggs and continue to get&nbsp;
fleshed out across the entire episode.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:51.480
After Riley takes Ellie into the abandoned&nbsp;
mall, Ellie marvels at the wonders of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:55.800
working escalator. What's playing in the&nbsp;
background? A-Ha's hit song, "Take On Me."

00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:00.300
Fans of "The Last of Us Part II" will recognize&nbsp;
the significance of the famous song right away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:04.740
as, in the game, Ellie plays an acoustic&nbsp;
rendition of the song to Dina in the music&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:08.760
store in Seattle. The song is an important&nbsp;
piece of Dina and Ellie's relationship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:12.720
and the showrunners opted to bridge that&nbsp;
into Riley and Ellie's relationship, too.

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:17.340
Just like in the "Left Behind" DLC, Ellie&nbsp;
and Riley dance and kiss to Etta James'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:21.660
"I Got You Babe." That song choice was likely&nbsp;
always going to find its way into the episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:25.374
but seeing it hinted at early&nbsp;
in the episode is a nice touch.

00:04:25.527 --> 00:04:29.040
As Riley and Ellie are making their way&nbsp;
into the abandoned mall in Episode 7,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:32.880
Ellie's flashlight starts flickering&nbsp;
uncontrollably. Ellie shakes her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:36.060
flashlight to get it working again,&nbsp;
much like the player would in the game.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:40.260
Whenever a flashlight starts to go bad in&nbsp;
the game, players have to gently "shake"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:44.100
their controller to get it working again.&nbsp;
Showing Ellie fix her faulty flashlight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:49.256
like this is a clever way to insert a common&nbsp;
gameplay moment into the narrative of the show.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:54.300
In the abandoned mall, there is a shot of a movie&nbsp;
poster for "Dawn of the Wolf Part II." In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:58.080
game, Joel reveals that "Dawn of the Wolf" was one&nbsp;
of the last movies he and Sarah watched together.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:02.880
"Dawn of the Wolf" is "The Last of Us" universe's&nbsp;
version of "Twilight," as Joel says that it's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:06.720
another "dumb teen movie" when Ellie asks if&nbsp;
the main character gets "gutted" at the end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:10.800
When "The Last of Us" was being developed&nbsp;
by Naughty Dog, "Twilight" was one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:15.300
biggest movie franchises in the world. But,&nbsp;
unlike "Twilight" and its five installments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:19.545
"Dawn of the Wolf" was limited to just&nbsp;
two entries before the world fell apart.

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:24.420
On the walls of Ellie's FEDRA room, there are&nbsp;
numerous space-themed posters, including one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:29.340
the moon, and a "Mortal Kombat II" poster, an&nbsp;
interest hinted at by Ellie in Episode 3 that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:33.540
will fully come to fruition later in Episode&nbsp;
7. There's a picture of a giraffe in there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:38.100
as well as a plethora of "Savage Starlight"&nbsp;
comic books, one of which Ellie reads before bed.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:42.180
On her bedside table, too, is Will&nbsp;
Livingston's "No Pun Intended Vol.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:45.960
1." We got to see Ellie reading from&nbsp;
"Volume Too" earlier in the season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:50.640
and it is Episode 7 where we learn that it was&nbsp;
Riley who gifted her the beloved second edition.

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:54.960
There are also dinosaur drawings, a hint&nbsp;
to a quick moment later in the episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:57.900
and a memorable scene in "The Last of Us Part II."

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:03.120
When Riley reveals that she has a gift for Ellie,&nbsp;
Ellie quickly begins a rapid-fire interrogation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:08.395
of Riley, begging to know what the gift is. At the&nbsp;
end of her rambling, she offers a final question:

00:06:08.395 --> 00:06:09.790
"Is it a dinosaur?"

00:06:09.790 --> 00:06:12.900
Fans of "The Last of Us Part II" will&nbsp;
remember that, during the first major&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:16.980
flashback in the game, Joel takes Ellie&nbsp;
to a surprise location for her birthday.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:21.164
Joel won't budge on it, even though Ellie&nbsp;
berates him with never-ending questions.

00:06:21.164 --> 00:06:23.024
"Is it a dinosaur?"

00:06:23.024 --> 00:06:27.720
And then, to her excitement, it is. Joel takes&nbsp;
Ellie to the Wyoming Museum of Science and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:32.369
History, which has a large, full-scale&nbsp;
statue of a Tyrannosaurus Rex outside.

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:37.500
One of the biggest changes in HBO's adaptation of&nbsp;
"The Last of Us" is the location where Joel and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:42.120
Ellie meet Henry and Sam. In the game, the meeting&nbsp;
takes place in Pittsburgh, but in the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:46.680
it's changed to Kansas City. Moving the location&nbsp;
doesn't have a huge impact on the narrative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:50.760
save for how Pittsburgh's yellow bridges were&nbsp;
used as a beacon of direction for Ellie and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:55.080
Joel in the game. However, Pittsburgh does not&nbsp;
get thrown to the curb entirely in the show.

00:06:55.080 --> 00:07:00.060
In Episode 8, Ellie and David take shelter in&nbsp;
a rundown barn. David tells Ellie that he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:04.560
part of the Pittsburgh quarantine zone until it&nbsp;
fell out of FEDRA's control. Viewers who haven't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:09.120
played the game won't pick up on the significance&nbsp;
of this, but die-hard fans of the franchise will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:13.200
be pleased to hear Pittsburgh get a mention.&nbsp;
David claiming that the city fell aligns with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:18.235
events in the canon, as Pittsburgh is overrun&nbsp;
with hunters who rule the streets in the game.

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:21.660
In "The Last of Us Part 2," Ellie&nbsp;
tells her girlfriend Dina that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:25.260
Joel's favorite movie franchise is a&nbsp;
cheesy fictional '80s action series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:29.458
and she wants to use it as an olive branch&nbsp;
to help ease their strained relationship.

00:07:29.576 --> 00:07:32.576
"What movie you guys gonna&nbsp;
watch? What's Joel into?"&nbsp;

00:07:33.274 --> 00:07:36.034
"Uh, Curtis and Viper 2."

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:40.440
Later in the game, while taking shelter&nbsp;
with Dina and Jesse in a Seattle theater,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:44.400
Ellie notices a "Curtis and Viper 4"&nbsp;
poster on the wall in one of the back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:47.520
rooms and makes a comment to herself&nbsp;
that it was one of Joel's favorites.

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:52.860
In the show, fans will notice that the DVD Sarah&nbsp;
borrows for Joel on his birthday in Episode 1 is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:57.420
"Curtis and Viper 2," a film that obviously&nbsp;
holds importance in their relationship. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.420 --> 00:08:00.960
what's the plot? In "The Last of Us&nbsp;
Part 2," Ellie explains that it's about,

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:06.180
"Two former commandos that go rogue to fight&nbsp;
bad guys. And I think in [Curtis and Viper 2],&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:09.060
they go up against Russian spies&nbsp;
or something. [...] The young one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.060 --> 00:08:12.776
Viper, he’s a trained ninja.&nbsp;
And he’s a complete badass."

00:08:12.776 --> 00:08:16.919
"Oh, this is the one with the deleted scenes!"
"Yeah, imagine how bad those have to be."

00:08:16.919 --> 00:08:20.520
This Easter egg makes the impact the&nbsp;
franchise has on Ellie and Joel in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:24.180
video game much more dynamic, as it&nbsp;
emphasizes what Ellie means to Joel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:27.180
and how she has become a surrogate&nbsp;
daughter to him after Sarah's death.

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:31.200
Joel is eventually willing to share his&nbsp;
interests with Ellie — interests that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:34.440
he once shared with Sarah, whose&nbsp;
absence he felt so much that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:37.500
could barely talk about her in&nbsp;
the original "The Last of Us."

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:41.940
When players wake up as Sarah in the video game,&nbsp;
they have the option to pick up a birthday card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:47.100
in her bedroom. The card reads "Congratulations!"&nbsp;
and features an illustration of a green cartoon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:51.540
dinosaur wearing a party hat. Sarah says&nbsp;
that she forgot to give the card to her dad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:55.020
but that worry is soon overshadowed&nbsp;
by the noises and chaos outside.

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:58.320
In Episode 1 of the show, there&nbsp;
is a shot of Sarah's bedroom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:01.800
and that exact same birthday card&nbsp;
can be briefly seen on her desk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:06.180
underneath some art supplies. The inclusion&nbsp;
of the art supplies in the scene could mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:11.040
that Sarah went to the trouble of hand-making her&nbsp;
father's card, a neat detail left out of the game.

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:15.720
Much like in the game, the shots of the birthday&nbsp;
card are not lingered on for very long. Sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:18.060
Sarah forgets to give it to&nbsp;
Joel after gifting him his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:20.880
repaired watch and falling asleep&nbsp;
while watching a movie together.

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:25.500
Another neat detail from the opening: Sarah&nbsp;
is wearing a t-shirt for the fictional band&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:30.960
"Halican Drops" in both the show and the game.&nbsp;
In both versions, the list of tour stops on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:35.120
back include several locations that will&nbsp;
be important to the story down the road.

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:39.420
One of the most infamous scenes from the&nbsp;
original game comes when Tommy, Joel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:43.860
and Sarah are attempting to drive out of Austin&nbsp;
amidst the outbreak. When they end up downtown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:48.660
Tommy's truck is totaled by another vehicle&nbsp;
as the infected attack survivors and fires&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:52.200
break out across town, which leads to&nbsp;
Sarah injuring her leg in the escape.

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:57.600
Fans were likely expecting that very same outcome&nbsp;
to unfold in the first episode of the show, given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:01.740
how pivotal this event is in "The Last of Us" and&nbsp;
the fact that the scene mirrors the perspective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:06.720
players experience in the game. Instead, the&nbsp;
writers changed the layout of the scene, opting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:10.980
to first fake audiences out with an oncoming&nbsp;
truck that just narrowly misses Tommy's truck.

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:14.100
The chaos that flips Tommy's truck in&nbsp;
the show does not stem from another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:19.500
road-bound vehicle. Instead, a plane crashes&nbsp;
in close proximity to Tommy, Joel, and Sarah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:24.180
which leads to debris flying at the truck&nbsp;
and causing it to crash. The switch-up gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:28.565
the scene a fresh feel in an episode that&nbsp;
was largely faithful to the original game.

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:33.180
Speaking of "faithful to the game" – here's an&nbsp;
easy-to-miss small detail that fans of the game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.180 --> 00:10:37.740
likely picked up on. When Joel is forced to kill&nbsp;
his infected neighbor with a wrench in Episode 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:41.460
he discards it, likely out of disgust&nbsp;
for what he just had to do with it.

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:45.780
But in the "The Last of Us" game series,&nbsp;
wrenches can be used as weapons, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:50.160
player is forced to discard them all the time,&nbsp;
since they only have a limited number of uses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:54.660
So switching out Joel killing his neighbor&nbsp;
Jimmy for Joel killing Nana, the mother of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:58.926
his neighbor Connie, was a fresh take that&nbsp;
also nodded at the mechanics of the game.

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:03.960
The world of "The Last of Us" is plunged into&nbsp;
chaos after the cordyceps fungal outbreak. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:08.460
the game, it begins with infected crops in South&nbsp;
America. However, the show takes one of the main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:12.900
sources of the spread — airborne spores — and&nbsp;
substitutes them with tendrils that grow out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:17.590
the infected. There must be some explanation for&nbsp;
how cordyceps took over the world in the show.

00:11:17.590 --> 00:11:19.020
"I was thinking we'd make some cookies!"&nbsp;

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:22.383
"Chocolate chip?"
"Raisin."

00:11:22.383 --> 00:11:25.920
Joel and Sarah manage to avoid eating&nbsp;
flour-based products at least four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:29.580
different times in Episode 1. First, they&nbsp;
find they have run out of it at breakfast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:33.720
and make eggs instead of pancakes. Later,&nbsp;
they politely decline when their neighbors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:37.920
offer them biscuits – the same biscuits&nbsp;
the infected neighbor ate. After that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:41.700
Sarah and Connie bake cookies together,&nbsp;
but Sarah doesn't take any because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:45.900
have raisins in them. Finally, Joel forgets&nbsp;
to pick up his birthday cake after work.

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:49.320
Beyond that, the creators of the show&nbsp;
may have hinted at all of this in "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:52.020
Last of Us Podcast." There,&nbsp;
Craig Mazin mentions that,

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:56.460
"Careful viewers of this episode will&nbsp;
be rewarded repeatedly because little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.460 --> 00:12:00.540
bits of breadcrumbs have been planted that are&nbsp;
going to pay off later in interesting ways."

00:12:00.540 --> 00:12:03.960
Plus, it just so happens that the&nbsp;
world's largest flour mill is in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:07.616
Jakarta, which appears to be at the&nbsp;
center of the outbreak on the show.

00:12:07.616 --> 00:12:10.980
"Jakarta. Where is that? Middle&nbsp;
East? Doesn't ring a bell."&nbsp;

00:12:10.980 --> 00:12:14.120
"Definitely a country. Maybe part of Asia?"
"Jakarta isn't a country."

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:19.320
The second episode of the series reveals the&nbsp;
first cases of the human variant of Cordyceps took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.320 --> 00:12:24.720
place near a flour factory in Jakarta. An episode&nbsp;
later, Joel explains to Ellie that it was indeed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:29.265
contaminated baking ingredients that helped the&nbsp;
outbreak spread throughout the world so quickly.

00:12:29.479 --> 00:12:32.385
"Dad, you love biscuits."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.734 --> 00:12:38.374
"I do … But I'm on Atkins."

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:42.780
When Ellie awakens at the beginning of&nbsp;
Episode 2, a butterfly hovers above her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:47.460
This is a direct callback to various scenes in&nbsp;
the original game, where Ellie is constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:51.900
enamored by the beauty of the butterflies and&nbsp;
fireflies living outside of the quarantine zone.

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:53.760
Butterflies become a symbol of hope,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:57.600
which mirrors the hope that the Fireflies&nbsp;
have in Ellie's resistance to the Cordyceps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.600 --> 00:13:01.946
virus -- which they think may hold&nbsp;
the key to producing a viable vaccine.

00:13:01.946 --> 00:13:03.420
"A vaccine."

00:13:03.420 --> 00:13:07.500
Any child born after Outbreak Day is&nbsp;
brought into a world of immense violence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:12.120
and cruelty. When Ellie is falling in love with&nbsp;
nature, taking the time to read comic books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:16.080
or cracking jokes about the adults, it's&nbsp;
a reminder that she's still just a kid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:20.100
trying to piece together some semblance&nbsp;
of fun amid a hollow and deadly reality.

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:25.320
On top of all that, before the events of "The Last&nbsp;
of Us Part II," Ellie gets a tattoo of a moth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:30.000
signifying the changes her character was starting&nbsp;
to develop compared to the butterfly imagery.

00:13:30.871 --> 00:13:34.160
"Um, just so it's out there, I can't swim."

00:13:34.160 --> 00:13:38.340
In the original game, it's a running joke&nbsp;
-- and nuisance -- that Ellie can't swim. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:42.960
numerous missions, Joel is forced to push Ellie&nbsp;
across bodies of water using a wooden pallet.

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:47.040
In "The Last of Us Part II," flashbacks&nbsp;
show that Joel teaches her to swim and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:51.240
she actually becomes quite skilled at it. She&nbsp;
even gets good enough to make a joke about her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:55.341
past shortcomings when the two encounter&nbsp;
a stray pallet near a museum in Wyoming.

00:13:55.341 --> 00:14:02.661
"Joel! Look! Wanna give me a ride?"
"Those days are long gone, kiddo."

00:14:02.661 --> 00:14:05.220
When Joel, Tess, and Ellie&nbsp;
reach the hotel in Boston,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:09.120
there is a lot of water separating them&nbsp;
and the stairwell they need to get to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.120 --> 00:14:12.900
Ellie says that she can't swim because there&nbsp;
aren't many pools in the quarantine zone.

00:14:12.900 --> 00:14:17.040
Annoyed by her point, Joel shows Ellie&nbsp;
that the water only goes up to his knees.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:20.700
Ellie's inability to swim isn't as much&nbsp;
of a plot point in the show as it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:24.715
in the game -- and those ubiquitous&nbsp;
pallets also never come into play.

00:14:24.900 --> 00:14:28.500
In the game, as Joel and Ellie are about&nbsp;
to reach the hospital in Salt Lake City&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:32.160
where the Fireflies are stationed, they&nbsp;
stumble upon a group of giraffes in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:36.060
open field. It's one of the most beautiful&nbsp;
and enchanting parts of the first game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:39.780
and it gives the player a break from&nbsp;
the never-ending brutality. Players&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.780 --> 00:14:42.873
can stand at the ledge and watch the&nbsp;
giraffes for as long as they'd like.

00:14:42.873 --> 00:14:50.303
"It's got its ups and downs. You&nbsp;
can't deny the view, though."

00:14:50.303 --> 00:14:54.720
In Episode 2 of the show, as Joel, Tess,&nbsp;
and Ellie are traveling across Boston and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.720 --> 00:14:58.460
heading toward the Capitol building, they&nbsp;
stop and take in the sights from a rooftop.

00:14:58.460 --> 00:14:59.818
"You can't deny that view."

00:15:01.260 --> 00:15:04.740
Fragments of the same composition that&nbsp;
soundtracked the giraffe encounter in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:08.880
the game are used to score this scene – a&nbsp;
subtle link between the game and the show.

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:11.580
In the finale, we get a live-action version of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:15.633
giraffe scene, a touching moment in&nbsp;
a heartbreaking and violent episode.

00:15:15.633 --> 00:15:18.133
"Is it everything you'd hoped for?"
&nbsp;

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:22.800
"It's&nbsp;got its ups and down. But&nbsp;
you can't deny that view."
 

